general_notes.general_notes:
This is a large-scale study (N=1030 children under 8 years old) from a single cohort (Shanghai Autism Early Development Cohort) examining brain structural asymmetry in very young children with ASD and DD/ID. The study is notable for: (1) focusing on a young age range (1-7 years) to minimize developmental and environmental effects; (2) separating ASD with DD/ID from ASD without DD/ID to examine comorbidities; (3) employing normative modeling to capture individual-level deviations and heterogeneity; (4) conducting multiscale cascade analysis linking genetic expression, brain structure, and clinical phenotypes; (5) using spatial permutation tests to control for spatial autocorrelation in brain-gene associations. The study population is entirely Chinese/Asian. Two scanner sites were used (Philips Ingenia and Siemens Verio) with ComBat harmonization. Sedation with chloral hydrate was used for most uncooperative children (only 2/563 ASD with DD/ID, 1/212 ASD without DD/ID, 1/36 DD/ID, and 21/219 TD were scanned without sedation). The discrepancy between 247 TD used for training and 219 TD in demographics is not clearly explained. The normative model was trained on 247 TD children but only 219 TD are described in the demographics table, suggesting possible exclusion or data splitting that is not fully detailed. Clinical assessments included ADOS, CARS, ABC, SM, SRS, GDS, and WPPSI/WISC-R. Gene expression data from Allen Human Brain Atlas (6 postmortem brains) was used, with only left hemisphere data analyzed. The study found that unsupervised clustering methods (k-means and t-SNE) could not separate the three patient groups based on GMV asymmetry patterns, supporting a continuum model rather than discrete subtypes.


